{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-7645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse  $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-36x+16y=-16$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-7645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-36x+16y & =-16 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+4(0)^{2}-36x+16(0) & =-16 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-36x & =-16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}+4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{36}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(2+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-7645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91d8efc1e91f1e1faa7b0f7fd10d4f526ff4b2a2536071a4ba68b8c0b50376f1"} {"question" "q-7645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-7645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)"] ["math" "(1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)"] ["math" "(2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)"] ["math" "(2\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-7645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-9466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-9466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10} & =1 \\\\ 2+(y-1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =1\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,1-2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,1+2\\sqrt{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-9466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91d8efc1e91f1e1faa7b0f7fd10d4f526ff4b2a2536071a4ba68b8c0b50376f1"} {"question" "q-9466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-9466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,1\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(0,1\\pm 3\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(0,\\pm 3)"] ["text" "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,5)$"] ["text" "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,4)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-9466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,1\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-70184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4x+54y+76=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-70184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4x+54y+76 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2}+4x+54\\cdot 0+76 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+76 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4+76 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+72 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+2)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that $(x+2)^{2}=-72.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-70184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91d8efc1e91f1e1faa7b0f7fd10d4f526ff4b2a2536071a4ba68b8c0b50376f1"} {"question" "q-70184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-70184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2-6\\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(-2\\pm 6\\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(-6\\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(\\pm 6\\sqrt{2},0)"] ["text" "There is no $x$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-70184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is no $x$-intercept"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2x-12\\sqrt{3}y+18=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2x-12\\sqrt{3}y+18 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2(0)-12\\sqrt{3}y+18 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}-12\\sqrt{3}y+18 & =0 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}y]+18 & =0 \\\\ 6[(y-\\sqrt{3})^{2}-3]+18 & =0 \\\\ 6(y-\\sqrt{3})^{2}-18+18 & =0 \\\\ 6(y-\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/assets/2d833daa50a68c13799763685b4ed961bd3d76d7708b6acb105b9eba6eb39211.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91d8efc1e91f1e1faa7b0f7fd10d4f526ff4b2a2536071a4ba68b8c0b50376f1"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-274495" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-328528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\frac{4}{5})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-328528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-8)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{64}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 144+25(y+1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ 25(y+1)^{2} & =81 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =\\frac{81}{25} \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ y & =-1\\pm \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{14}{5},\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\frac{4}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,-\\frac{14}{5}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-328528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91d8efc1e91f1e1faa7b0f7fd10d4f526ff4b2a2536071a4ba68b8c0b50376f1"} {"question" "q-328528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-328528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6fb8ca0e42a673551639226c33d841943e1a93137ac92a44513628a0025a58e"} {"question" "q-328528" "field" "fiel